The semantics, verified by the module system in use:

```js
// math.js — named exports + one default per module
export const PI = 3.14159;
export function square(x) { return x * x; }
export default class Calculator {}   // at most ONE default

// app.js
import Calc, { PI, square } from './math.js';  // default first, no braces
import * as math from './math.js';               // namespace import
import { PI as TAU } from './math.js';            // rename on import
// ESM is static: import paths are strings, resolved and linked BEFORE execution
```

The ESM vs CommonJS table (the interview core):

```
                ESM                     CommonJS
syntax          import / export          require / module.exports
resolution      STATIC (parse-time)      DYNAMIC (runtime, any expression)
loading         async, parallel         sync, blocking
value binding   LIVE view of export     COPY at require-time
this at top     undefined               exports/{} object
files           .mjs (or type:module)   .cjs (default in Node)
hoisting        imports hoisted         require runs in place
```

The live-binding difference (the follow-up that tests depth):
```js
// ESM: counter.js
export let count = 0;
export function inc() { count++; }
// importer sees count CHANGE as inc() is called — the import IS the binding

// CommonJS: counter.cjs
let count = 0; module.exports = { count, inc };  // count COPIED at require
// importer's count stays 0 forever — it got a snapshot
```

Why browsers/Node both moved to ESM (the ecosystem answer): static imports enable tree-shaking (bundlers PROVE which exports are unused and delete them), async loading enables parallel fetch of the module graph, and one module format across browser/Node/tools replaced the UMD/AMD era. package.json type: module flips a project to ESM; the practical rule: new code is ESM, CommonJS interop via import cjs from './legacy.cjs' works (the default export = module.exports).
